ion.\n1180 | \n1181 |     metric : str or callable, default=\"euclidean\"\n1182 |         The metric to use when calculating distance between instances in a\n1183 |         feature array. If metric is a string, it must be one of the options\n1184 |         specified in PAIRED_DISTANCES, including \"euclidean\",\n1185 |         \"manhattan\", or \"cosine\".\n1186 |         Alternatively, if metric is a callable function, it is called on each\n1187 |         pair of instances (rows) and the resulting value recorded. The callable\n1188 |         should take two arrays from `X` as input and return a value indicating\n1189 |         the distance between them.\n1190 | \n1191 |     **kwds : dict\n1192 |         Unused parameters.\n1193 | \n1194 |     Returns\n1195 |     -------\n1196 |     distances : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n1197 |         Returns the distances between the row vectors of `X`\n1198 |         and the row vectors of `Y`.\n1199 | \n1200 |     See Also\n1201 |     --------\n1202 |     pairwise_distances : Computes the distance between every pair of samples.\n1203 | \n1204 |     Examples\n1205 |     --------\n1206 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import paired_distances\n1207 |     >>> X = [[0, 1], [1, 1]]\n1208 |     >>> Y = [[0, 1], [2, 1]]\n1209 |     >>> paired_distances(X, Y)\n1210 |     array([0., 1.])\n1211 |     \"\"\"\n1212 | \n1213 |     if metric in PAIRED_DISTANCES:\n1214 |         func = PAIRED_DISTANCES[metric]\n1215 |         return func(X, Y)\n1216 |     elif callable(metric):\n1217 |         # Check the matrix first (it is usually done by the metric)\n1218 |         X, Y = check_paired_arrays(X, Y)\n1219 |         distances = np.zeros(len(X))\n1220 |         for i in range(len(X)):\n1221 |             distances[i] = metric(X[i], Y[i])\n1222 |         return distances\n1223 |     else:\n1224 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown distance %s\" % metric)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:linear_kernel`.\nSource: Retri