r of connected components, as computed by\n105 |         `scipy.sparse.csgraph.connected_components`.\n106 | \n107 |     component_labels : array of shape (n_samples)\n108 |         Labels of connected components, as computed by\n109 |         `scipy.sparse.csgraph.connected_components`.\n110 | \n111 |     mode : {'connectivity', 'distance'}, default='distance'\n112 |         Type of graph matrix: 'connectivity' corresponds to the connectivity\n113 |         matrix with ones and zeros, and 'distance' corresponds to the distances\n114 |         between neighbors according to the given metric.\n115 | \n116 |     metric : str\n117 |         Metric used in `sklearn.metrics.pairwise.pairwise_distances`.\n118 | \n119 |     kwargs : kwargs\n120 |         Keyword arguments passed to\n121 |         `sklearn.metrics.pairwise.pairwise_distances`.\n122 | \n123 |     Returns\n124 |     -------\n125 |     graph : sparse matrix of shape (n_samples, n_samples)\n126 |         Graph of connection between samples, with a single connected component.\n127 |     \"\"\"\n128 |     if metric == \"precomputed\" and sparse.issparse(X):\n129 |         raise RuntimeError(\n130 |             \"_fix_connected_components with metric='precomputed' requires the \"\n131 |             \"full distance matrix in X, and does not work with a sparse \"\n132 |             \"neighbors graph.\"\n133 |         )\n134 | \n135 |     for i in range(n_connected_components):\n136 |         idx_i = np.flatnonzero(component_labels == i)\n137 |         Xi = X[idx_i]\n138 |         for j in range(i):\n139 |             idx_j = np.flatnonzero(component_labels == j)\n140 |             Xj = X[idx_j]\n141 | \n142 |             if metric == \"precomputed\":\n143 |                 D = X[np.ix_(idx_i, idx_j)]\n144 |             else:\n145 |                 D = pairwise_distances(Xi, Xj, metric=metric, **kwargs)\n146 | \n147 |             ii, jj = np.unravel_index(D.argmin(axis=None), D.shape)\n148 |             if mode == \"connectivity\":\n149 |                 graph[idx_i[ii], idx_j[jj]] = 1\n150 |                 graph[idx_j[jj], idx_i[ii]] = 1\n151 |             elif mode == \"distance\":\n152 |                 graph[idx_i[ii], idx_j[jj]] = D[ii, jj]\n153 |                 graph[idx_j[jj], idx_i[ii]] = D[ii, jj]\n154 |             else:\n155 |                 raise ValueError(\n156 |                     \"Unknown mode=%r, should be one of ['connectivity', 'distance'].\"\n157 |                     % mode\n158 |                 )\n159 | \n160 |     return graph\n```\n\nFound function `examples/applications/wikipedia_principal_eigenvector.py:get_redirects`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 91 | def get_redirects(redirects_filename):\n 92 |     \"\"\"Parse the redirections and build a transitively closed map out of it\"\"\"\n 93 |     redirects = {}\n 94 |     print(\"Parsing the NT redirect file\")\n 95 |     for l, line in enumerate(BZ2File(redirects_filename)):\n 96 |         split = line.split()\n 97 |         if len(split) != 4:\n 98 |             print(\"ignoring malformed line: \" + line)\n 99 |             continue\n100 |         redirects[short_name(split[0])] = short_name(split[2])\n101 |         if l % 1000000 == 0:\n102 |             print(\"[%s] line: %08d\" % (datetime.now().isoformat(), l))\n103 | \n104 |     # compute the transitive closure\n105 |     print(\"Computing the transitive closure of the redirect relation\")\n106 |     for l, source in enumerate(redirects.keys()):\n107 |         transitive_target = None\n108 |         target = redirects[source]\n109 |         seen = {source}\n110 |         while True:\n111 |             transitive_target = target\n112 |             target = redirects.get(target)\n113 |             if target is None or target in seen:\n114 |                 break\n115 |             seen.add(target)\n116 |         redirects[source] = transitive_target\n117 |         if l % 1000000 == 0:\n118 |             print(\"[%s] line: %08d\" % (datetime.now().isoformat(), l))\n119 | \n120 |     return redirects\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also check for any utility functions that might be relevant:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['safe_sparse_dot', 'check_array']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"safe_sparse_dot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `safe_sparse_dot`.\n```\n160 | def safe_sparse_dot(a, b, *, dense_output=False):\n161 |     \"\"\"Dot product that handle the sparse matrix case correctly.\n162 | \n163 |     Parameters\n164 |     ----------\n165 |     a : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n166 |     b : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n167 |     dense_output : bool, default=False\n168 |         When False, ``a`` and ``b`` both being sparse will yield sparse output.\n169 |         When True, output will always be a dense array.\n170 | \n171 |     Returns\n172 |     -------\n173 |     dot_product : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n174 |         Sparse if ``a`` and ``b`` are sparse and ``dense_output=False``.\n175 |     \"\"\"\n176 |     if a.ndim > 2 or b.ndim > 2:\n177 |         if sparse.issparse(a):\n178 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies b is 3D+\n179 |             # [i, j] @ [k, ..., l, m, n] -> [i, k, ..., l, n]\n180 |             b_ = np.rollaxis(b, -2)\n181 |             b_2d = b_.reshape((b.shape[-2], -1))\n182 |             ret = a @ b_2d\n183 |             ret = ret.reshape(a.shape[0], *b_.shape[1:])\n184 |         elif sparse.issparse(b):\n185 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies a is 3D+\n186 |             # [k, ..., l, m] @ [i, j] -> [k, ..., l, j]\n187 |             a_2d = a.reshape(-1, a.shape[-1])\n188 |             ret = a_2d @ b\n189 |             ret = ret.reshape(*a.shape[:-1], b.shape[1])\n190 |         else:\n191 |             ret = np.dot(a, b)\n192 |     else:\n193 |         ret = a @ b\n194 | \n195 |     if (\n196 |         sparse.issparse(a)\n197 |         and sparse.issparse(b)\n198 |         and dense_output\n199 |         and hasattr(ret, \"toarray\")\n200 |     ):\n201 |         return ret.toarray()\n202 |     return ret\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"check_array\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `check_array`.\n```\n 651 | def check_array(\n 652 |     array,\n 653 |     accept_sparse=False,\n 654 |     *,\n 655 |     accept_large_sparse=True,\n 656 |     dtype=\"numeric\",\n 657 |     order=None,\n 658 |     copy=False,\n 659 |     force_all_finite=True,\n 660 |     ensure_2d=True,\n 661 |     allow_nd=False,\n 662 |     ensure_min_samples=1,\n 663 |     ensure_min_features=1,\n 664 |     estimator=None,\n 665 |     input_name=\"\",\n 666 | ):\n 667 |     \"\"\"Input validation on an array, list, sparse matrix or similar.\n 668 | \n 669 |     By default, the input is checked to be a non-empty 2D array containing\n 670 |     only finite values. If the dtype of the array is object, attempt\n 671 |     converting to float, raising on failure.\n 672 | \n 673 |     Parameters\n 674 |     ----------\n 675 |     array : object\n 676 |         Input object to check / convert.\n 677 | \n 678 |     accept_sparse : str, bool or list/tuple of str, default=False\n 679 |         String[s] representing allowed sparse matrix formats, such as 'csc',\n 680 |         'csr', etc. If the input is sparse but not in the allowed format,\n 681 |         it will be converted to the first listed format. True allows the input\n 682 |         to be any format. False means that a sparse matrix input will\n 683 |         raise an error.\n 684 | \n 685 |     accept_large_sparse : bool, default=True\n 686 |         If a CSR, CSC, COO or BSR sparse matrix is supplied and accepted by\n 687 |         accept_sparse, accept_large_sparse=False will cause it to be accepted\n 688 |         only if its indices are stored with a 32-bit dtype.\n 689 | \n 690 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 691 | \n 692 |     dtype : 'numeric', type, list of type or None, default='numeric'\n 693 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n 694 |         If \"numeric\", dtype is preserved unless array.dtype is object.\n 695 |         If dtype is a list of types, conversion on the first type is only\n 696 |         performed if the dtype of the input is not in the list.\n 697 | \n 698 |     order : {'F', 'C'} or None, default=None\n 699 |         Whether an array will be forced to be fortran or c-style.\n 700 |         When order is None (default), then if copy=False, nothing is ensured\n 701 |         about the memory layout of the output array; otherwise (copy=True)\n 702 |         the memory layout of the returned array is kept as close as possible\n 703 |         to the original array.\n 704 | \n 705 |     copy : bool, default=False\n 706 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n 707 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n 708 | \n 709 |     force_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 710 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n 711 |         possibilities are:\n 712 | \n 713 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n 714 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n 715 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n 716 |           cannot be infinite.\n 717 | \n 718 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 719 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n 720 | \n 721 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 722 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n 723 | \n 724 |     ensure_2d : bool, default=True\n 725 |         Whether to raise a value error if array is not 2D.\n 726 | \n 727 |     allow_nd : bool, default=False\n 728 |         Whether to allow array.ndim > 2.\n 729 | \n 730 |     ensure_min_samples : int, default=1\n 731 |         Make sure that the array has a minimum number of samples in its first\n 732 |         axis (rows for a 2D array). Setting to 0 disables this check.\n 733 | \n 734 |     ensure_min_features : int, default=1\n 735 |         Make sure that the 2D array has some minimum number of features\n 736 |         (columns). The default value of 1 rejects empty datasets.\n 737 |         This check is only enforced when the input data has effectively 2\n 738 |         dimensions or is originally 1D and ``ensure_2d`` is True. Setting to 0\n 739 |         disables this check.\n 740 | \n 741 |     estimator : str or estimator instance, default=None\n 742 |         If passed, include the name of the estimator in warning messages.\n 743 | \n 744 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n 745 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n 746 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n 747 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n 748 |         documentation.\n 749 | \n 750 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 751 | \n 752 |     Returns\n 753 |     -------\n 754 |     array_converted : object\n 755 |         The converted and validated array.\n 756 |     \"\"\"\n 757 |     if isinstance(array, np.matrix):\n 758 |         raise TypeError(\n 759 |             \"np.matrix is not supported. Please convert to a numpy array with \"\n 760 |             \"np.asarray. For more information see: \"\n 761 |             \"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.matrix.html\"\n 762 |         )\n 763 | \n 764 |     xp, is_array_api_compliant = get_namespace(array)\n 765 | \n 766 |     # store reference to original array to check if copy is needed when\n 767 |     # function returns\n 768 |     arr