ws the input\n419 |         to be any format. False means that a sparse matrix input will\n420 |         raise an error.\n421 | \n422 |     accept_large_sparse : bool, default=True\n423 |         If a CSR, CSC, COO or BSR sparse matrix is supplied and accepted by\n424 |         accept_sparse, accept_large_sparse=False will cause it to be accepted\n425 |         only if its indices are stored with a 32-bit dtype.\n426 | \n427 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n428 | \n429 |     dtype : string, type, list of types or None, default=\"numeric\"\n430 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n431 |         If \"numeric\", dtype is preserved unless array.dtype is object.\n432 |         If dtype is a list of types, conversion on the first type is only\n433 |         performed if the dtype of the input is not in the list.\n434 | \n435 |     order : 'F', 'C' or None, default=None\n436 |         Whether an array will be forced to be fortran or c-style.\n437 |         When order is None (default), then if copy=False, nothing is ensured\n438 |         about the memory layout of the output array; otherwise (copy=True)\n439 |         the memory layout of the returned array is kept as close as possible\n440 |         to the original array.\n441 | \n442 |     copy : boolean, default=False\n443 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n444 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n445 | \n446 |     force_all_finite : boolean or 'allow-nan', default=True\n447 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n448 |         possibilities are:\n449 | \n450 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n451 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n452 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n453 |           cannot be infinite.\n454 | \n455 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n456 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n457 | \n458 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n459 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n460 | \n461 |     ensure_2d : boolean, default=True\n462 |         Whether to raise a value error if array is not 2D.\n463 | \n464 |     allow_nd : boolean, default=False\n465 |         Whether to allow array.ndim > 2.\n466 | \n467 |     ensure_min_samples : int, default=1\n468 |         Make sure that the array has a minimum number of samples in its first\n469 |         axis (rows for a 2D array). Setting to 0 disables this check.\n470 | \n471 |     ensure_min_features : int, default=1\n472 |         Make sure that the 2D array has some minimum number of features\n473 |         (columns). The default value of 1 rejects empty datasets.\n474 |         This check is only enforced when the input data has effectively 2\n475 |         dimensions or is originally 1D and ``ensure_2d`` is True. Setting to 0\n476 |         disables this check.\n477 | \n478 |     estimator : str or estimator instance, default=None\n479 |         If passed, include the name of the estimator in warning messages.\n480 | \n481 |     Returns\n482 |     -------\n483 |     array_converted : object\n484 |         The converted and validated array.\n485 |     \"\"\"\n486 |     # store reference to original array to check if copy is needed when\n487 |     # function returns\n488 |     array_orig = array\n489 | \n490 |     # store whether originally we wanted numeric dtype\n491 |     dtype_numeric = isinstance(dtype, str) and dtype == \"numeric\"\n492 | \n493 |     dtype_orig = getattr(array, \"dtype\", None)\n494 |     if not hasattr(dtype_orig, 'kind'):\n495 |         # not a data type (e.g. a column named dtype in a pandas DataFrame)\n496 |         dtype_orig = None\n497 | \n498 |     # check if the object contains several dtypes (typically a pandas\n499 |     # DataFrame), and store them. If not, store None.\n500 |     dtypes_orig = None\n501 |     has_pd_integer_ar