colors_with_alphas = list(zip(facecolors, face_alphas))\n37 | edgecolors_with_alphas = list(zip(edgecolors, edge_alphas))\n38 | \n39 | ax2.bar(x_values, y_values, color=colors_with_alphas,\n40 |         edgecolor=edgecolors_with_alphas)\n41 | ax2.set_title('Normalized alphas for\\neach bar and each edge')\n42 | \n43 | plt.show()\n44 | \n45 | # %%\n46 | #\n47 | # .. admonition:: References\n48 | #\n49 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n50 | #    in this example:\n51 | #\n52 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar`\n53 | #    - `matplotlib.pyplot.subplots`\n54 | #\n55 | # .. tags::\n56 | #\n57 | #    styling: color\n58 | #    plot-type: bar\n59 | #    level: beginner\n60 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/statistics/histogram_multihist.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =====================================================\n  3 | The histogram (hist) function with multiple data sets\n  4 | =====================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | Plot histogram with multiple sample sets and demonstrate:\n  7 | \n  8 | * Use of legend with multiple sample sets\n  9 | * Stacked bars\n 10 | * Step curve with no fill\n 11 | * Data sets of different sample sizes\n 12 | \n 13 | Selecting different bin counts and sizes can significantly affect the\n 14 | shape of a histogram. The Astropy docs have a great section on how to\n 15 | select these parameters:\n 16 | http://docs.astropy.org/en/stable/visualization/histogram.html\n 17 | \n 18 | .. redirect-from:: /gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/filled_step\n 19 | \n 20 | \"\"\"\n 21 | # %%\n 22 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 23 | import numpy as np\n 24 | \n 25 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n 26 | \n 27 | n_bins = 10\n 28 | x = np.random.randn(1000, 3)\n 29 | \n 30 | fig, ((ax0, ax1), (ax2, ax3)) = plt.subplots(nrows=2, ncols=2)\n 31 | \n 32 | colors = ['red', 'tan', 'lime']\n 33 | ax0.hist(x, n_bins, density=True, histtype='bar', color=colors, label=colors)\n 34 | ax0.legend(prop={'size': 10})\n 35 | ax0.set_title('bars with legend')\n 36 | \n 37 | ax1.hist(x, n_bins, density=True, histtype='bar', stacked=True)\n 38 | ax1.set_title('stacked bar')\n 39 | \n 40 | ax2.hist(x, n_bins, histtype='step', stacked=True, fill=False)\n 41 | ax2.set_title('stack step (unfilled)')\n 42 | \n 43 | # Make a multiple-histogram of data-sets with different length.\n 44 | x_multi = [np.random.randn(n) for n in [10000, 5000, 2000]]\n 45 | ax3.hist(x_multi, n_bins, histtype='bar')\n 46 | ax3.set_title('different sample sizes')\n 47 | \n 48 | fig.tight_layout()\n 49 | plt.show()\n 50 | \n 51 | # %%\n 52 | # -----------------------------------\n 53 | # Setting properties for each dataset\n 54 | # -----------------------------------\n 55 | #\n 56 | # You can style the histograms individually by passing a list of values to the\n 57 | # following parameters:\n 58 | #\n 59 | # * edgecolor\n 60 | # * facecolor\n 61 | # * hatch\n 62 | # * linewidth\n 63 | # * linestyle\n 64 | #\n 65 | #\n 66 | # edgecolor\n 67 | # .........\n 68 | \n 69 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 70 | \n 71 | edgecolors = ['green', 'red', 'blue']\n 72 | \n 73 | ax.hist(x, n_bins, fill=False, histtype=\"step\", stacked=True,\n 74 |         edgecolor=edgecolors, label=edgecolors)\n 75 | ax.legend()\n 76 | ax.set_title('Stacked Steps with Edgecolors')\n 77 | \n 78 | plt.show()\n 79 | \n 80 | # %%\n 81 | # facecolor\n 82 | # .........\n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.hist`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n6728 | def hist(self, x, bins=None, range=None, density=False, weights=None,\n6729 |              cumulative=False, bottom=None, histtype='bar', align='mid',\n6730 |              orientation='vertical', rwidth=None, log=False,\n6731 |              color=None, label=None, stacked=False, **kwargs):\n6732 |         \"\"\"\n6733 |         Compute and plot a histogram.\n6734 | \n6735 |         This method uses `numpy.histogram` to bin the data in *x* and count the\n6736 |         number of values in each bin, then draws the distribution either as a\n6737 |         `.BarContainer` or `.Polygon`. The *bins*, *range*, *density*, and\n6738 |         *weights* parameters are forwarded to `numpy.histogram`.\n6739 | \n6740 |         If the data has already been binned and counted, use `~.bar` or\n6741 |         `~.stairs` to plot the distribution::\n6742 | \n6743 |             counts, bins = np.histogram(x)\n6744 |             plt.stairs(counts, bins)\n6745 | \n6746 |         Alternatively, plot pre-computed bins and counts using ``hist()`` by\n6747 |         treating each bin as a single point with a weight equal to its count::\n6748 | \n6749 |             plt.hist(bins[:-1], bins, weights=counts)\n6750 | \n6751 |         The data input *x* can be a singular array, a list of datasets of\n6752 |         potentially different lengths ([*x0*, *x1*, ...]), or a 2D ndarray in\n6753 |         which each column is a dataset. Note that the ndarray form is\n6754 |         transposed relative to the list form. If the input is an array, then\n6755 |         the return value is a tuple (*n*, *bins*, *patches*); if the input is a\n6756 |         sequence of arrays, then the return value is a tuple\n6757 |         ([*n0*, *n1*, ...], *bins*, [*patches0*, *patches1*, ...]).\n6758 | \n6759 |         Masked arrays are not supported.\n6760 | \n6761 |         Parameters\n6762 |         ----------\n6763 |         x : (n,) array or sequence of (n,) arrays\n6764 |             Input values, this takes either a single array or a sequence of\n6765 |             arrays which are not required to be of the same length.\n6766 | \n6767 |         bins : int or sequence or str, default: :rc:`hist.bins`\n6768 |             If *bins* is an integer, it defines the number of equal-width bins\n6769 |             in the range.\n6770 | \n6771 |             If *bins* is a sequence, it defines the bin edges, including the\n6772 |             left edge of the first bin and the right edge of the last bin;\n6773 |             in this case, bins may be unequally spaced.  All but the last\n6774 |             (righthand-most) bin is half-open.  In other words, if *bins* is::\n6775 | \n6776 |                 [1, 2, 3, 4]\n6777 | \n6778 |             then the first bin is ``[1, 2)`` (including 1, but excluding 2) and\n6779 |             the second ``[2, 3)``.  The last bin, however, is ``[3, 4]``, which\n6780 |             *includes* 4.\n6781 | \n6782 |             If *bins* is a string, it is one of the binning strategies\n6783 |             supported by `numpy.histogram_bin_edges`: 'auto', 'fd', 'doane',\n6784 |             'scott', 'stone', 'rice', 'sturges', or 'sqrt'.\n6785 | \n6786 |         range : tuple or None, default: None\n6787 |             The lower and upper range of the bins. Lower and upper outliers\n6788 |             are ignored. If not provided, *range* is ``(x.min(), x.max())``.\n6789 |             Range has no effect if *bins* is a sequence.\n6790 | \n6791 |             If *bins* is a sequence or *range* is specified, autoscaling\n6792 |             is based on the specified bin range instead of the\n6793 |             range of x.\n6794 | \n6795 |         density : bool, default: False\n6796 |             If ``True``, draw and return a probability density: each bin\n6797 |             will display the bin's raw count divided by the total number of\n6798 |             counts *and the bin width*\n6799 |             (``density = counts / (sum(counts) * np.diff(bins))``),\n6800 |             so that the area under the histogram integrates to 1\n6801 |             (``np.sum(density * np.diff(bins)) == 1``).\n6802 | \n6803 |             If *stacked* is also ``True``, the sum of the histograms is\n6804 |             normalized to 1.\n6805 | \n6806 |         weights : (n,) array-like or None, default: None\n6807 |             An array of weights, of the same shape as *x*.  Each value in\n6808 |             *x* only contributes its associated weight towards the bin count\n6809 |             (instead of 1).  If *density* is ``True``, the weights are\n6810 |             normalized, so that the integral of the density over the range\n6811 |             remains 1.\n6812 | \n6813 |         cumulative : bool or -1, default: False\n6814 |             If ``True``, then a histogram is computed where each bin gives the\n6815 |             counts in that bin plus all bins for smaller values. The last bin\n6816 |             gives the total number of datapoints.\n6817 | \n6818 |             If *density* is also ``True`` then the histogram is normalized such\n6819 |             that the last bin equals 1.\n6820 | \n6821 |             If *cumulative* is a number less than 0 (e.g., -1), the direction\n6822 |             of accumulation is reversed.  In this case, if *density* is also\n6823 |             ``True``, then the histogram is normalized such that the first bin\n6824 |             equals 1.\n6825 | \n6826 |         bottom : array-like, scalar, or None, default: None\n6827 |             Location of the bottom of each bin, i.e. bins are drawn from\n6828 |             ``bottom`` to ``bottom + hist(x, bins)`` If a scalar, the bottom\n6829 |             of each bin is shifted by the same amount. If an array, each bin\n6830 |             is shifted independently and the length of bottom must match the\n6831 |             number of bins. If None, defaults to 0.\n6832 | \n6833 |         histtype : {'bar', 'barstacked', 'step', 'stepfilled'}, default: 'bar'\n6834 |             The type of histogram to draw.\n6835 | \n6836 |             - 'bar' is a traditional bar-type histogram.  If multiple data\n6837 |               are given the bars are arranged side by side.\n6838 |             - 'barstacked' is a bar-type histogram where multiple\n6839 |               data are stacked on top of each other.\n6840 |             - 'step' generates a lineplot that is by default unfilled.\n6841 |             - 'stepfilled' generates a lineplot that is by default filled.\n6842 | \n6843 |         align : {'left', 'mid', 'right'}, default: 'mid'\n6844 |             The horizontal alignment of the histogram bars.\n6845 | \n6846 |             - 'left': bars are centered on the left bin edges.\n6847 |             - 'mid': bars are centered between the bin edges.\n6848 |             - 'right': bars are centered on the right bin edges.\n6849 | \n6850 |         orientation : {'vertical', 'horizontal'}, default: 'vertical'\n6851 |             If 'horizontal', `~.Axes.barh` will be used for bar-type histograms\n6852 |             and the *bottom* kwarg will be the left edges.\n6853 | \n6854 |         rwidth : float or None, default: None\n6855 |             The relative width of the bars as a fraction of the bin width.  If\n6856 |             ``None``, automatically compute the width.\n6857 | \n6858 |             Ignored if *histtype* is 'step' or 'stepfilled'.\n6859 | \n6860 |         log : bool, default: False\n6861 |             If ``True``, the histogram axis will be set to a log scale.\n6862 | \n6863 |         color : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color` or None, default: None\n6864 |             Color or sequence of colors, one per dataset.  Default (``None``)\n6865 |             uses the standard line color sequence.\n6866 | \n6867 |         label : str or list of str, optional\n6868 |             String, or sequence of strings to match multiple datasets.  Bar\n6869 |             charts yield multiple patches per dataset, but only the first gets\n6870 |             the label, so that `~.Axes.legend` will work as expected.\n6871 | \n6872 |         stacked : bool, default: False\n6873 |             If ``True``, multiple data are stacked on top of each other If\n6874 |             ``False`` multiple data are arranged side by side if histtype is\n6875 |             'bar' or on top of each other if histtype is 'step'\n6876 | \n6877 |         Returns\n6878 |         -------\n6879 |         n : array or list of arrays\n6880 |             The values of the histogram bins. See *density* and *weights* for a\n6881 |             description of the possible semantics.  If input *x* is an array,\n6882 |             then this is an array of length *nbins*. If input is a sequence of\n6883 |             arrays ``[data1, data2, ...]``, then this is a list of arrays with\n6884 |             the values of the histograms for each of the arrays in the same\n6885 |             order.  The dtype of the array *n* (or of its element arrays) will\n6886 |             always be float even if no weighting or normalization is used.\n6887 | \n6888 |         bins : array\n6889 |             The edges of the bins. Length nbins + 1 (nbins left edges and right\n6890 |             edge of last bin).  Always a single array even when multiple data\n6891 |             sets are passed in.\n6892 | \n6893 |         patches : `.BarContainer` or list of a single `.Polygon` or list of \\\n6894 | such objects\n6895 |             Container of individual artists used to create the histogram\n6896 |             or list of such containers if there are multiple input datasets.\n6897 | \n6898 |         Other Parameters\n6899 |         ----------------\n6900 |         data : indexable object, optional\n6901 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n6902 | \n6903 |         **kwargs\n6904 |             `~matplotlib.patches.Patch` properties. The following properties\n6905 |             additionally accept a sequence of values corresponding to the\n6906 |             datasets in *x*:\n6907 |             *edgecolor*, *facecolor*, *linewidth*, *linestyle*, *hatch*.\n6908 | \n6909 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n6910 |                Allowing sequences of values in above listed Patch properties.\n6911 | \n6912 |         See Also\n6913 |         --------\n6914 |         hist2d : 2D histogram with rectangular bins\n6915 |         hexbin : 2D histogram with hexagonal bins\n6916 |         stairs : Plot a pre-computed histogram\n6917 |         bar : Plot a pre-computed histogram\n6918 | \n6919 |         Notes\n6920 |         -----\n6921 |         For large numbers of bins (>1000), plotting can be significantly\n6922 |         accelerated by using `~.Axes.stairs` to plot a pre-computed histogram\n6923 |         (``plt.stairs(*np.histogram(data))``), or by setting *histtype* to\n6924 |         'step' or 'stepfilled' rather than 'bar' or 'barstacked'.\n6925 |         \"\"\"\n6926 |         # Avoid shadowing the builtin.\n6927 |         bin_range = range\n6928 |         from builtins import range\n6929 | \n6930 |         if np.isscalar(x):\n6931 |             x = [x]\n6932 | \n6933 |         if bins is None:\n6934 |             bins = mpl.rcParams['hist.bins']\n6935 | \n6936 |         # Validate string inputs here to avoid cluttering subsequent code.\n6937 |         _api.check_in_list(['bar', 'barstacked', 'step', 'stepfilled'],\n6938 |                            histtype=histtype)\n6939 |         _api.check_in_list(['left', 'mid', 'right'], align=align)\n6940 |         _api.check_in_list(['horizontal', 'vertical'], orientation=orientation)\n6941 | \n6942 |         if histtype == 'barstacked' and not stacked:\n6943 |             stacked = True\n6944 | \n6945 |         # Massage 'x' for processing.\n6946 |         x = cbook._reshape_2D(x, 'x')\n6947 |         nx = len(x)  # number of datasets\n6948 | \n6949 |         # Process unit information.  _process_unit_info sets the unit and\n6950 |         # converts the first dataset; then we convert each following dataset\n6951 |         # one at a time.\n6952 |         if orientation == \"vertical\":\n6953 |             convert_units = self.convert_xunits\n6954 |             x = [*self._process_unit_info([(\"x\", x[0])], kwargs),\n6955 |                  *map(convert_units, x[1:])]\n6956 |         else:  # horizontal\n6957 |             convert_units = self.convert_yunits\n6958 |             x = [*self._process_unit_info([(\"y\", x[0])], kwargs),\n6959 |                  *map(convert_units, x[1:])]\n6960 | \n6961 |         if bin_range is not None:\n6962 |             bin_range = convert_units(bin_range)\n6963 | \n6964 |         if not cbook.is_scalar_or_string(bins):\n6965 |             bins = convert_units(bins)\n6966 | \n6967 |         # We need to do to 'weights' what was done to 'x'\n6968 |         if weights is not None:\n6969 |             w = cbook._reshape_2D(weights, 'weights')\n6970 |         else:\n6971 |             w = [None] * nx\n6972 | \n6973 |         if len(w) != nx:\n6974 |             raise ValueError('weights should have the same shape as x')\n6975 | \n6976 |         input_empty = True\n6977 |         for xi, wi in zip(x, w):\n6978 |             len_xi = len(xi)\n6979 |             if wi is not None and len(wi) != len_xi:\n6980 |                 raise ValueError('weights should have the same shape as x')\n6981 |             if len_xi:\n6982 |                 input_empty = False\n6983 | \n6984 |         if color is None:\n6985 |             colors = [self._get_lines.get_next_color() for i in range(nx)]\n6986 |         else:\n6987 |             colors = mcolors.to_rgba_array(color)\n6988 |             if len(colors) != nx:\n6989 |                 raise ValueError(f\"The 'color' keyword argument must have one \"\n6990 |                                  f\"color per dataset, but {nx} datasets and \"\n6991 |                                  f\"{len(colors)} colors were provided\")\n6992 | \n6993 |         hist_kwargs = dict()\n6994 | \n6995 |         # if the bin_range is not given, compute without nan numpy\n6996 |         # does not do this for us when guessing the range (but will\n6997 |         # happily ignore nans when computing the histogram).\n6998 |         if bin_range is None:\n6999 |             xmin = np.inf\n7000 |             xmax = -np.inf\n7001 |             for xi in x:\n7002 |                 if len(xi):\n7003 |                     # python's min/max ignore nan,\n7004 |                     # np.minnan returns nan for all nan input\n7005 |                     xmin = min(xmin, np.nanmin(xi))\n7006 |                     xmax = max(xmax, np.nanmax(xi))\n7007 |             if xmin <= xmax:  # Only happens if we have seen a finite value.\n7008 |                 bin_range = (xmin, xmax)\n7009 | \n7010 |         # If bins are not specified either explicitly or via range,\n7011 |         # we need to figure out the range required for all datasets,\n7012 |         # and supply that to np.histogram.\n7013 |         if not input_empty and len(x) > 1:\n7014 |             if weights is not None:\n7015 |                 _w = np.concatenate(w)\n7016 |             else:\n7017 |                 _w = None\n7018 |             bins = np.histogram_bin_edges(\n7019 |                 np.concatenate(x), bins, bin_range, _w)\n7020 |         else:\n7021 |             hist_kwargs['range'] = bin_range\n7022 | \n7023 |         density = bool(density)\n7024 |         if density and not stacked:\n7025 |             hist_kwargs['density'] = density\n7026 | \n7027 |         # List to store all the top coordinates of the histograms\n7028 |         tops = []  # Will have shape (n_datasets, n_bins).\n7029 |         # Loop through datasets\n7030 |         for i in range(nx):\n7031 |             # this will automatically overwrite bins,\n7032 |             # so that each histogram uses the same bins\n7033 |             m, bins = np.histogram(x[i], bins, weights=w[i], **hist_kwargs)\n7034 |             tops.append(m)\n7035 |         tops = np.array(tops, float)  # causes problems later if it's an int\n7036 |         bins = np.array(bins, float)  # causes problems if float16\n7037 |         if stacked:\n7038 |             tops = tops.cumsum(axis=0)\n7039 |             # If a stacked density plot, normalize so the area of all the\n7040 |             # stacked histograms together is 1\n7041 |             if density:\n7042 |                 tops = (tops / np.diff(bins)) / tops[-1].sum()\n7043 |         if cumulative:\n7044 |             slc = slice(None)\n7045 |             if isinstance(cumulative, Number) and cumulative < 0:\n7046 |                 slc = slice(None, None, -1)\n7047 |             if density:\n7048 |                 tops = (tops * np.diff(bins))[:, slc].cumsum(axis=1)[:, slc]\n7049 |             else:\n7050 |                 tops = tops[:, slc].cumsum(axis=1)[:, slc]\n7051 | \n7052 |         patches = []\n7053 | \n7054 |         if histtype.startswith('bar'):\n7055 | \n7056 |             totwidth = np.diff(bins)\n7057 | \n7058 |             if rwidth is not None:\n7059 |                 dr = np.clip(rwidth, 0, 1)\n7060 |             elif (len(tops) > 1 and\n7061 |                   ((not stacked) or mpl.rcParams['_internal.classic_mode'])):\n7062 |                 dr = 0.8\n7063 |             else:\n7064 |                 dr = 1.0\n7065 | \n7066 |             if histtype == 'bar' and not stacked:\n7067 |                 width = dr * totwidth / nx\n7068 |                 dw = width\n7069 |                 boffset = -0.5 * dr * totwidth * (1 - 1 / nx)\n7070 |             elif histtype == 'barstacked' or stacked:\n7071 |                 width = dr * totwidth\n7072 |                 boffset, dw = 0.0, 0.0\n7073 | \n7074 |             if align == 'mid':\n7075 |                 boffset += 0.5 * totwidth\n7076 |             elif align == 'right':\n7077 |                 boffset += totwidth\n7078 | \n7079 |             if orientation == 'horizontal':\n7080 |                 _barfunc = self.barh\n7081 |                 bottom_kwarg = 'left'\n7082 |             else:  # orientation == 'vertical'\n7083 |                 _barfunc = self.bar\n7084 |                 bottom_kwarg = 'bottom'\n7085 | \n7086 |             for top, color in zip(tops, colors):\n7087 |                 if bottom is None:\n7088 |                     bottom = np.zeros(len(top))\n7089 |                 if stacked:\n7090 |                     height = top - bottom\n7091 |                 else:\n7092 |                     height = top\n7093 |                 bars = _barfunc(bins[:-1]+boffset, height, width,\n7094 |                                 align='center', log=log,\n7095 |                                 color=color, **{bottom_kwarg: bottom})\n7096 |                 patches.append(bars)\n7097 |                 if stacked:\n7098 |                     bottom = top\n7099 |                 boffset += dw\n7100 |             # Remove stickies from all bars but the lowest ones, as otherwise\n7101 |             # margin expansion would be unable to cross the stickies in the\n7102 |             # middle of the bars.\n7103 |             for bars in patches[1:]:\n7104 |                 for patch in bars:\n7105 |                     patch.sticky_edges.x[:] = patch.sticky_edges.y[:] = []\n7106 | \n7107 |         elif histtype.startswith('step'):\n7108 |             # these define the perimeter of the polygon\n7109 |             x = np.zeros(4 * len(bins) - 3)\n7110 |             y = np.zeros(4 * len(bins) - 3)\n7111 | \n7112 |             x[0:2*len(bins)-1:2], x[1:2*len(bins)-1:2] = bins, bins[:-1]\n7113 |             x[2*len(bins)-1:] = x[1:2*len(bins)-1][::-1]\n7114 | \n7115 |             if bottom is None:\n7116 |                 bottom = 0\n7117 | \n7118 |             y[1:2*len(bins)-1:2] = y[2:2*len(bins):2] = bottom\n7119 |             y[2*len(bins)-1:] = y[1:2*len(bins)-1][::-1]\n7120 | \n7121 |             if log:\n7122 |                 if orientation == 'horizontal':\n7123 |                     self.set_xscale('log', nonpositive='clip')\n7124 |                 else:  # orientation == 'vertical'\n7125 |                     self.set_yscale('log', nonpositive='clip')\n7126 | \n7127 |             if align == 'left':\n7128 |                 x -= 0.5*(bins[1]-bins[0])\n7129 |             elif align == 'right':\n7130 |                 x += 0.5*(bins[1]-bins[0])\n7131 | \n7132 |             # If fill kwarg is set, it will be passed to the patch collection,\n7133 |             # overriding this\n7134 |             fill = (histtype == 'stepfilled')\n7135 | \n7136 |             xvals, yvals = [], []\n7137 |             for top in tops:\n7138 |                 if stacked:\n7139 |                     # top of the previous polygon becomes the bottom\n7140 |                     y[2*len(bins)-1:] = y[1:2*len(bins)-1][::-1]\n7141 |                 # set the top of this polygon\n7142 |                 y[1:2*len(bins)-1:2] = y[2:2*len(bins):2] = top + bottom\n7143 | \n7144 |                 # The starting point of the polygon has not yet been\n7145 |                 # updated. So far only the endpoint was adjusted. This\n7146 |                 # assignment closes the polygon. The redundant endpoint is\n7147 |                 # later discarded (for step and stepfilled).\n7148 |                 y[0] = y[-1]\n7149 | \n7150 |                 if orientation == 'horizontal':\n7151 |                     xvals.append(y.copy())\n7152 |                     yvals.append(x.copy())\n7153 |                 else:\n7154 |                     xvals.append(x.copy())\n7155 |                     yvals.append(y.copy())\n7156 | \n7157 |             # stepfill is closed, step is not\n7158 |             split = -1 if fill else 2 * len(bins)\n7159 |             # add patches in reverse order so that when stacking,\n7160 |             # items lower in the stack are plotted on top of\n7161 |             # items higher in the stack\n7162 |             for x, y, color in reversed(list(zip(xvals, yvals, colors))):\n7163 |                 patches.append(self.fill(\n7164 |                     x[:split], y[:split],\n7165 |                     closed=True if fill else None,\n7166 |                     facecolor=color,\n7167 |                     edgecolor=None if fill else color,\n7168 |                    