 for separate groups.\n152 |     grid : bool, default True\n153 |         Whether to show axis grid lines.\n154 |     xlabelsize : int, default None\n155 |         If specified changes the x-axis label size.\n156 |     xrot : float, default None\n157 |         Rotation of x axis labels. For example, a value of 90 displays the\n158 |         x labels rotated 90 degrees clockwise.\n159 |     ylabelsize : int, default None\n160 |         If specified changes the y-axis label size.\n161 |     yrot : float, default None\n162 |         Rotation of y axis labels. For example, a value of 90 displays the\n163 |         y labels rotated 90 degrees clockwise.\n164 |     ax : Matplotlib axes object, default None\n165 |         The axes to plot the histogram on.\n166 |     sharex : bool, default True if ax is None else False\n167 |         In case subplots=True, share x axis and set some x axis labels to\n168 |         invisible; defaults to True if ax is None otherwise False if an ax\n169 |         is passed in.\n170 |         Note that passing in both an ax and sharex=True will alter all x axis\n171 |         labels for all subplots in a figure.\n172 |     sharey : bool, default False\n173 |         In case subplots=True, share y axis and set some y axis labels to\n174 |         invisible.\n175 |     figsize : tuple, optional\n176 |         The size in inches of the figure to create. Uses the value in\n177 |         `matplotlib.rcParams` by default.\n178 |     layout : tuple, optional\n179 |         Tuple of (rows, columns) for the layout of the histograms.\n180 |     bins : int or sequence, default 10\n181 |         Number of histogram bins to be used. If an integer is given, bins + 1\n182 |         bin edges are calculated and returned. If bins is a sequence, gives\n183 |         bin edges, including left edge of first bin and right edge of last\n184 |         bin. In this case, bins is returned unmodified.\n185 | \n186 |     backend : str, default None\n187 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n188 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n189 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n190 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n191 | \n192 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n193 | \n194 |     legend : bool, default False\n195 |         Whether to show the legend.\n196 | \n197 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n198 | \n199 |     **kwargs\n200 |         All other plotting keyword arguments to be passed to\n201 |         :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.hist`.\n202 | \n203 |     Returns\n204 |     -------\n205 |     matplotlib.AxesSubplot or numpy.ndarray of them\n206 | \n207 |     See Also\n208 |     --------\n209 |     matplotlib.pyplot.hist : Plot a histogram using matplotlib.\n210 | \n211 |     Examples\n212 |     --------\n213 |     This example draws a histogram based on the length and width of\n214 |     some animals, displayed in three bins\n215 | \n216 |     .. plot::\n217 |         :context: close-figs\n218 | \n219 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n220 |         ...     'length': [1.5, 0.5, 1.2, 0.9, 3],\n221 |         ...     'width': [0.7, 0.2, 0.15, 0.2, 1.1]\n222 |         ...     }, index=['pig', 'rabbit', 'duck', 'chicken', 'horse'])\n223 |         >>> hist = df.hist(bins=3)\n224 |     \"\"\"\n225 |     plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(backend)\n226 |     return plot_backend.hist_frame(\n227 |         data,\n228 |         column=column,\n229 |         by=by,\n230 |         grid=grid,\n231 |         xlabelsize=xlabelsize,\n232 |         xrot=xrot,\n233 |         ylabelsize=ylabelsize,\n234 |         yrot=yrot,\n235 |         ax=ax,\n236 |         sharex=sharex,\n237 |         sharey=sharey,\n238 |         figsize=figsize,\n239 |         layout=layout,\n240 |         legend=legend,\n241 |         bins=bins,\n242 |         **kwargs,\n243 |     )\n```\n"}, {"content": "After a thorough analysis of the codebase and dependencies, I 