keyword will change behavior in pandas 3.0.\n1137 |                 `Copy-on-Write\n1138 |                 <https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/user_guide/copy_on_write.html>`__\n1139 |                 will be enabled by default, which means that all methods with a\n1140 |                 `copy` keyword will use a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n1141 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n1142 |                 future version of pandas.\n1143 | \n1144 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n1145 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n1146 | \n1147 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n1148 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1149 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Series\n1150 |             or DataFrame.\n1151 | \n1152 |         Returns\n1153 |         -------\n1154 |         DataFrame, or None\n1155 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1156 | \n1157 |         See Also\n1158 |         --------\n1159 |         Series.rename : Alter Series index labels or name.\n1160 |         DataFrame.rename : Alter DataFrame index labels or name.\n1161 |         Index.rename : Set new names on index.\n1162 | \n1163 |         Notes\n1164 |         -----\n1165 |         ``DataFrame.rename_axis`` supports two calling conventions\n1166 | \n1167 |         * ``(index=index_mapper, columns=columns_mapper, ...)``\n1168 |         * ``(mapper, axis={'index', 'columns'}, ...)``\n1169 | \n1170 |         The first calling convention will only modify the names of\n1171 |         the index and/or the names of the Index object that is the columns.\n1172 |         In this case, the parameter ``copy`` is ignored.\n1173 | \n1174 |         The second calling convention will modify the names of the\n1175 |         corresponding index if mapper is a list or a scalar.\n1176 |         However, if mapper is dict-like or a function, it will use the\n1177 |         deprecated behavior of modifying the axis *labels*.\n1178 | \n1179 |         We *highly* recommend using keyword arguments to clarify your\n1180 |         intent.\n1181 | \n1182 |         Examples\n1183 |         --------\n1184 |         **DataFrame**\n1185 | \n1186 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1187 |         ...     {\"num_legs\": [4, 4, 2], \"num_arms\": [0, 0, 2]}, [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]\n1188 |         ... )\n1189 |         >>> df\n1190 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1191 |         dog            4         0\n1192 |         cat            4         0\n1193 |         monkey         2         2\n1194 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"animal\")\n1195 |         >>> df\n1196 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1197 |         animal\n1198 |         dog            4         0\n1199 |         cat            4         0\n1200 |         monkey         2         2\n1201 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"limbs\", axis=\"columns\")\n1202 |         >>> df\n1203 |         limbs   num_legs  num_arms\n1204 |         animal\n1205 |         dog            4         0\n1206 |         cat            4         0\n1207 |         monkey         2         2\n1208 | \n1209 |         **MultiIndex**\n1210 | \n1211 |         >>> df.index = pd.MultiIndex.from_product(\n1212 |         ...     [[\"mammal\"], [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]], names=[\"type\", \"name\"]\n1213 |         ... )\n1214 |         >>> df\n1215 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1216 |         type   name\n1217 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1218 |                cat            4         0\n1219 |                monkey         2         2\n1220 | \n1221 |         >>> df.rename_axis(index={\"type\": \"class\"})\n1222 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1223 |         class  name\n1224 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1225 |                cat            4         0\n1226 |                monkey         2         2\n1227 | \n1228 |         >>> df.rename_axis(columns=str.upper)\n1229 |         LIMBS          num_legs  num_arms\n1230 |         type   name\n1231 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1232 |                cat            4         0\n1233 |                monkey         2         2\n1234 |         \"\"\"\n1235 |         self._check_copy_deprecation(copy)\n1236 |         axes = {\"index\": index, \"columns\": columns}\n1237 | \n1238 |         if axis is not None:\n1239 |             axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n1240 | \n1241 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n1242 | \n1243 |         if mapper is not lib.no_default:\n1244 |             # Use v0.23 behavior if a scalar or list\n1245 |             non_mapper = is_scalar(mapper) or (\n1246 |                 is_list_like(mapper) and not is_dict_like(mapper)\n1247 |             )\n1248 |             if non_mapper:\n1249 |                 return self._set_axis_name(mapper, axis=axis, inplace=inplace)\n1250 |             else:\n1251 |                 raise ValueError(\"Use `.rename` to alter labels with a mapper.\")\n1252 |         else:\n1253 |             # Use new behavior.  Means that index and/or columns\n1254 |             # is specified\n1255 |             result = self if inplace else self.copy(deep=False)\n1256 | \n1257 |             for axis in range(self._AXIS_LEN):\n1258 |                 v = axes.get(self._get_axis_name(axis))\n1259 |                 if v is lib.no_default:\n1260 |                     continue\n1261 |                 non_mapper = is_scalar(v) or (is_list_like(v) and not is_dict_like(v))\n1262 |                 if non_mapper:\n1263 |                     newnames = v\n1264 |                 else:\n1265 |                     f = common.get_rename_function(v)\n1266 |                     curnames = self._get_axis(axis).names\n1267 |                     newnames = [f(name) for name in curnames]\n1268 |                 result._set_axis_name(newnames, axis=axis, inplace=True)\n1269 |             if not inplace:\n1270 |                 return result\n1271 |             return None\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n142 | def create_subplots(\n143 |     naxes: int,\n144 |     sharex: bool = False,\n145 |     sharey: bool = False,\n146 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n147 |     subplot_kw=None,\n148 |     ax=None,\n149 |     layout=None,\n150 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n151 |     **fig_kw,\n152 | ):\n153 |     \"\"\"\n154 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n155 | \n156 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n157 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n158 | \n159 |     Parameters\n160 |     ----------\n161 |     naxes : int\n162 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n163 |       nrows * ncols.\n164 | \n165 |     sharex : bool\n166 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n167 | \n168 |     sharey : bool\n169 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n170 | \n171 |     squeeze : bool\n172 | \n173 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n174 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n175 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n176 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n177 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n178 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n179 | \n180 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n181 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n182 | \n183 |     subplot_kw : dict\n184 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n185 |       subplots.\n186 | \n187 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n188 | \n189 |     layout : tuple\n190 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n191 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n192 | \n193 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n194 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n195 | \n196 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n197 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n198 |         automatically included here.\n199 | \n200 |     Returns\n201 |     -------\n202 |     fig, ax : tuple\n203 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n204 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n205 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n206 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n207 | \n208 |     Examples\n209 |     --------\n210 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n211 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n212 | \n213 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n214 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n215 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n216 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n217 | \n218 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n219 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n220 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n221 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n222 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n223 | \n224 |     # Four polar axes\n225 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n226 |     \"\"\"\n227 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n228 | \n229 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n230 |         subplot_kw = {}\n231 | \n232 |     if ax is None:\n233 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n234 |     else:\n235 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n236 |             if squeeze:\n237 |                 ax = np.fromiter(flatten_axes(ax), dtype=object)\n238 |             if layout is not None:\n239 |                 warnings.warn(\n240 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n241 |                     UserWarning,\n242 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n243 |                 )\n244 |             if sharex or sharey:\n245 |                 warnings.warn(\n246 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n247 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n248 |                     UserWarning,\n249 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n250 |                 )\n251 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n252 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 raise ValueError(\n256 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n257 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n258 |                 )\n259 | \n260 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n261 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n262 |         if naxes == 1:\n263 |             if squeeze:\n264 |                 return fig, ax\n265 |             else:\n266 |                 return fig, np.fromiter(flatten_axes(ax), dtype=object)\n267 |         else:\n268 |             warnings.warn(\n269 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n270 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n271 |                 UserWarning,\n272 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n273 |             )\n274 |             fig.clear()\n275 | \n276 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n277 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n278 | \n279 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n280 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n281 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n282 | \n283 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n284 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n285 | \n286 |     if sharex:\n287 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n288 |     if sharey:\n289 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n290 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n291 | \n292 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n293 |     # convention.\n294 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n295 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n296 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n297 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n298 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n299 |         if i >= naxes:\n300 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n301 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n302 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n303 |         axarr[i] = ax\n304 | \n305 |     if naxes != nplots:\n306 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n307 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n308 | \n309 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n310 | \n311 |     if squeeze:\n312 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n313 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n314 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n315 |         if nplots == 1:\n316 |             axes = axarr[0]\n317 |         else:\n318 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n319 |     else:\n320 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n321 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n322 | \n323 |     return fig, axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame._rename`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 998 | def _rename(\n 999 |         self,\n1000 |         mapper: Renamer | None = None,\n1001 |         *,\n1002 |         index: Renamer | None = None,\n1003 |         columns: Renamer | None = None,\n1004 |         axis: Axis | None = None,\n1005 |         inplace: bool = False,\n1006 |         level: Level | None = None,\n1007 |         errors: str = \"ignore\",\n1008 |     ) -> Self | None:\n1009 |         # called by Series.rename and DataFrame.rename\n1010 | \n1011 |         if mapper is None and index is None and columns is None:\n1012 |             raise TypeError(\"must pass an index to rename\")\n1013 | \n1014 |         if index is not None or columns is not None:\n1015 |             if axis is not None:\n1016 |                 raise TypeError(\n1017 |                     \"Cannot specify both 'axis' and any of 'index' or 'columns'\"\n1018 |                 )\n1019 |             if mapper is not None:\n1020 |                 raise TypeError(\n1021 |                     \"Cannot specify both 'mapper' and any of 'index' or 'columns'\"\n1022 |                 )\n1023 |         else:\n1024 |             # use the mapper argument\n1025 |             if axis and self._get_axis_number(axis) == 1:\n1026 |                 columns = mapper\n1027 |             else:\n1028 |                 index = mapper\n1029 | \n1030 |         self._check_inplace_and_allows_duplicate_labels(inplace)\n1031 |         result = self if inplace else self.copy(deep=False)\n1032 | \n1033 |         for axis_no, replacements in enumerate((index, columns)):\n1034 |             if replacements is None:\n1035 |                 continue\n1036 | \n1037 |             ax = self._get_axis(axis_no)\n1038 |             f = common.get_rename_function(replacements)\n1039 | \n1040 |             if level is not None:\n1041 |                 level = ax._get_level_number(level)\n1042 | \n1043 |             # GH 13473\n1044 |             if not callable(replacements):\n1045 |                 if ax._is_multi and level is not None:\n1046 |                     indexer = ax.get_level_values(level).get_indexer_for(replacements)\n1047 |                 else:\n1048 |                     indexer = ax.get_indexer_for(replacements)\n1049 | \n1050 |                 if errors == \"raise\" and len(indexer[indexer == -1]):\n1051 |                     missing_labels = [\n1052 |                         label\n1053 |                         for index, label in enumerate(replacements)\n1054 |                         if indexer[index] == -1\n1055 |                     ]\n1056 |                     raise KeyError(f\"{missing_labels} not found in axis\")\n1057 | \n1058 |             new_index = ax._transform_index(f, level=level)\n1059 |             result._set_axis_nocheck(new_index, axis=axis_no, inplace=True)\n1060 | \n1061 |         if inplace:\n1062 |             self._update_inplace(result)\n1063 |             return None\n1064 |         else:\n1065 |             return result.__finalize__(self, method=\"rename\")\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/internals/blocks.py:Block.astype`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n583 | def astype(\n584 |         self,\n585 |         dtype: DtypeObj,\n586 |         errors: IgnoreRaise = \"raise\",\n587 |         squeeze: bool = False,\n588 |     ) -> Block:\n589 |         \"\"\"\n590 |         Coerce to the new dtype.\n591 | \n592 |         Parameters\n593 |         ----------\n594 |         dtype : np.dtype or ExtensionDtype\n595 |         errors : str, {'raise', 'ignore'}, default 'raise'\n596 |             - ``raise`` : allow exceptions to be raised\n597 |             - ``ignore`` : suppress exceptions. On error return original object\n598 |         squeeze : bool, default False\n599 |             squeeze values to ndim=1 if only one column is given\n600 | \n601 |         Returns\n602 |         -------\n603 |         Block\n604 |         \"\"\"\n605 |         values = self.values\n606 |         if squeeze and values.ndim == 2 and is_1d_only_ea_dtype(dtype):\n607 |             if values.shape[0] != 1:\n608 |                 raise ValueError(\"Can not squeeze with more than one column.\")\n609 |             values = values[0, :]  # type: ignore[call-overload]\n610 | \n611 |         new_values = astype_array_safe(values, dtype, errors=errors)\n612 | \n613 |         new_values = maybe_coerce_values(new_values)\n614 | \n615 |         refs = None\n616 |         if astype_is_view(values.dtype, new_values.dtype):\n617 |             refs = self.refs\n618 | \n619 |         newb = self.make_block(new_values, refs=refs)\n620 |         if newb.shape != self.shape:\n621 |             raise TypeError(\n622 |                 f\"cannot set astype for dtype \"\n623 |                 f\"({self.dtype.name} [{self.shape}]) to different shape \"\n624 |                 f\"({newb.dtype.name} [{newb.shape}])\"\n625 |             )\n626 |         return newb\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's analyze the trace artifacts to understand the usage patterns of the squeeze method:\n\n<function=get_trace_artifacts>\n<parameter=path_contains>generic.py</parameter>\n<parameter=name_contains>squeeze</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_trace_artifacts", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n{\n  \"instance_id\": \"pandas-dev__pandas-60415\",\n  \"trace_summary\": {\n    \"unique_function_count\": 4,\n    \"returned_count\": 1,\n    \"filtered_count\": 1\n  },\n  \"applied_filters\": {\n    \"artifact_type\": \"all\",\n    \"path_contains\": \"generic.py\",\n    \"name_contains\": \"squeeze\",\n    \"start\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 8\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NDFrame.squeeze\",\n      \"path\": \"pandas/core/generic.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 841,\n      \"end_line\": 953,\n      \"event_count\": 10,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 2\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": false,\n  \"next_start\": 1\n}"}, {"content": "Let's also check the data dependencies to understand the relationships:\n\n<functio