re returned as\n7405 |         well, but not used for ordering.\n7406 | \n7407 |         This method is equivalent to\n7408 |         ``df.sort_values(columns, ascending=False).head(n)``, but more\n7409 |         performant.\n7410 | \n7411 |         Parameters\n7412 |         ----------\n7413 |         n : int\n7414 |             Number of rows to return.\n7415 |         columns : label or list of labels\n7416 |             Column label(s) to order by.\n7417 |         keep : {'first', 'last', 'all'}, default 'first'\n7418 |             Where there are duplicate values:\n7419 | \n7420 |             - ``first`` : prioritize the first occurrence(s)\n7421 |             - ``last`` : prioritize the last occurrence(s)\n7422 |             - ``all`` : keep all the ties of the smallest item even if it means\n7423 |               selecting more than ``n`` items.\n7424 | \n7425 |         Returns\n7426 |         -------\n7427 |         DataFrame\n7428 |             The first `n` rows ordered by the given columns in descending\n7429 |             order.\n7430 | \n7431 |         See Also\n7432 |         --------\n7433 |         DataFrame.nsmallest : Return the first `n` rows ordered by `columns` in\n7434 |             ascending order.\n7435 |         DataFrame.sort_values : Sort DataFrame by the values.\n7436 |         DataFrame.head : Return the first `n` rows without re-ordering.\n7437 | \n7438 |         Notes\n7439 |         -----\n7440 |         This function cannot be used with all column types. For example, when\n7441 |         specifying columns with `object` or `category` dtypes, ``TypeError`` is\n7442 |         raised.\n7443 | \n7444 |         Examples\n7445 |         --------\n7446 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n7447 |         ...     {\n7448 |         ...         \"population\": [\n7449 |         ...             59000000,\n7450 |         ...             65000000,\n7451 |         ...             434000,\n7452 |         ...             434000,\n7453 |         ...      